<tr><td>Metal panel</td><td>Lifted panels, failed fasteners, dents from hail</td><td>Cosmetic damage exclusions; panel availability</td></tr>
<tr><td>Low-slope (TPO, modified bitumen, built-up)</td><td>Punctures, seam separation, wind uplift at edges</td><td>Prior leaks; extent of wet insulation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tile</td><td>Cracked or displaced tiles</td><td>Matching and breakage during repair</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For storm-specific details, see our <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> pages.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/adjuster-measuring-roof-damage.webp" alt="Adjuster measuring storm damage on a shingle roof" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Measurements, slope counts, and close-up photos let an estimate reflect the whole roof, not just the obvious damage.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Repair or Replace?</h2>
<p>Insurers often start with a repair estimate. Whether that is enough depends on facts the estimate may not address:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Brittleness.</strong> Older shingles may crack when lifted, so a spot repair can cause new damage.</li>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> Discontinued shingle colors or profiles may not blend with existing slopes. Some policies limit what they owe for matching.</li>
<li><strong>Extent of damage per slope.</strong> Scattered damage across every slope is different from one blown-off corner.</li>
<li><strong>Manufacturer and code requirements.</strong> Installation instructions and the 2021 International Residential Code, which Port Arthur enforces, can affect underlayment, fastening, and flashing.</li>
<li><strong>Windstorm standards.</strong> Repairs and re-roofs in the TWIA catastrophe area must meet TDI specifications and be inspected to receive a WPI-8 certificate (<a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/wind/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TDI Windstorm Inspection Program</a>).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Permits, Codes, and What the Policy Pays</h2>
<p>The City of Port Arthur generally requires a permit for re-roofing and structural repairs. Permit techs can be reached at (409) 983-8261, and applications go through the city’s online permit portal (<a href="https://www.portarthurtx.gov/502/Inspections-and-Permitting" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Inspections and Permitting</a>). In cities that adopted TDI’s standard, proof of windstorm inspection enrollment may be needed before a building permit is issued.</p>
<p>Meeting code does not automatically mean the insurer pays for upgrades. Ordinance or law coverage, if you have it, pays up to its limit for increased costs to comply with codes. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page explains how these rules fit together.</p>

