<h2>How Roof Claims Get Reduced</h2>
<p>Even when a roof claim is approved, the check can be much smaller than the roofer’s bid. Common reasons include:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Reduction</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Wind/hail or named-storm deductible</td><td>Often a percentage of the dwelling limit, not a flat dollar amount</td></tr>
<tr><td>Depreciation (ACV)</td><td>Age-based reduction; recoverable later under replacement cost policies after repairs</td></tr>
<tr><td>ACV-only roof endorsement or payment schedule</td><td>Depreciation may never be paid back</td></tr>
<tr><td>Missing line items</td><td>Drip edge, starter, ridge, flashing, vents, decking, steep or high charges, permits</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wrong measurements</td><td>Undercounted squares, pitch, or waste factor</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If you already have the carrier’s estimate, an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> compares it line by line with the roof’s actual condition. Additional damage found during tear-off can be submitted as an <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplement</a>. If the dispute is over the amount of loss, <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> may be an option under your policy.</p>

<h2>Protecting a Damaged Roof</h2>
<p>Most policies require reasonable steps to prevent further damage. In Port Arthur, an open roof in summer can lead to wet insulation and mold within days. Photograph the damage before any tarp goes on, keep receipts for emergency repairs, and do not discard damaged shingles or decking until they have been documented. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lists what to save.</p>

<h2>Older Roofs and Prior Repairs</h2>
<p>Many Port Arthur homes are on their second or third roof since Rita, Ike, and Laura, and some have had patch repairs after several smaller storms. That history matters on a new claim. Insurers may ask for the roof’s age, prior claim records, and proof that earlier damage was repaired. Keep invoices, permits, and WPI-8 certificates from past roof work in one place. If an earlier claim paid for a roof that was never replaced, expect the insurer to ask about it. Being able to show the roof was in sound condition before the most recent storm, with dated photos or a recent inspection, can make a real difference when the carrier argues that damage is old.</p>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster Can Help With Roof Claims</h2>
<p>A public adjuster represents the policyholder, not the insurer. On a roof claim, that may include a full roof inspection with measurements and photos, a line-item Xactimate estimate that includes code and windstorm items where supported, a review of your policy’s roof endorsements, and communication with the insurer’s adjuster. A Port Arthur Public Adjuster does not perform or sell repairs on the claims we adjust. No one can guarantee a full roof replacement. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
<p>We serve Port Arthur, Jefferson County, and surrounding Southeast Texas communities, including <a href="/service-areas/nederland/">Nederland</a>, Port Neches, Groves, and Beaumont.</p>
